add('Conductor 1680 collapses to eight order30 phases with multiplicity48','Conductor bridge','2026-10-16','T','Cyclotomic trace arithmetic',
'Which exact field and phase multiplicities underlie the conductor bridge, without assuming an unrecovered E₈ matrix intertwiner?',
'The retrieved source specifies ω=ζ₁₆₈₀ and η=ω⁵⁶=ζ₃₀. This is enough to prove the phase and trace multiplicities directly. It does not by itself identify an integral lattice or replay the claimed E₈ coordinate matrices.',
['Let ζ_N=exp(2πi/N), K=Q(ζ₁₆₈₀) and L=Q(ζ₃₀). The primitive phases are ζ_Nᵃ for gcd(a,N)=1.','Define c₃₀(n)=Σ_(a mod30,gcd(a,30)=1)ζ₃₀^(an), the Ramanujan sum.'],
'Exact degree and trace transport',
'φ(1680)=384, φ(30)=8 and [K:L]=48. The map ζ₁₆₈₀ᵃ↦ζ₃₀ᵃ sends the384 primitive phases onto the eight primitive order30 phases with exactly48 preimages each. Consequently Tr_(K/Q)(ω^(56n))=48c₃₀(n).',
'The elementary totient product gives φ(1680)=1680(1−1/2)(1−1/3)(1−1/5)(1−1/7)=384 and φ(30)=8. Because η=ω⁵⁶ has order30, L⊆K; cyclotomic irreducibility and the tower law give extension degree48. Reduction of units modulo1680 to units modulo30 is surjective: a prescribed residue coprime to30 can be extended to an odd residue modulo16 and a nonzero residue modulo7 using the Chinese remainder theorem. Its fibres are cosets of the kernel and therefore all have size384/8=48. Summing ω^(56an)=ζ₃₀^(an) over the384 embeddings of K groups the terms into these equal fibres, proving the trace identity. Equivalently, an element of L has relative trace48 times itself before taking its trace to Q.',
'Compute a trace with n coprime to30.',
'Möbius inclusion–exclusion gives c₃₀(1)=μ(30)=−1, hence Tr_(K/Q)(ω⁵⁶)=−48. At n=30, every primitive η contributes one, so c₃₀(30)=8 and the K-trace is384. At n=2, the divisor formula gives c₃₀(2)=−1+2=1, so the larger-field trace is48. At n=15, every primitive order30 phase has fifteenth power−1, giving c₃₀(15)=−8 and trace−384. The verifier independently enumerates the reduced residue fibres as integer classes.',
'The eight-phase reduction and48-fold trace scale are exact arithmetic facts.',
'The equations BᵀAB=G and CⁿB=BDⁿ require the actual matrices to prove an integral E₈ identification; equal dimension or trace data alone do not establish it. No such matrix certificate is claimed replayed here.',
'Recover the proposed lattice basis B, verify its Gram matrix and the one-step intertwining CB=BD over integers, then use induction to obtain every power and compare lattice integrality separately from rational representation equivalence.',
[('NIST DLMF: Ramanujan sums and finite Fourier series','https://dlmf.nist.gov/27.10')])
add('A 264-fold root filter differs from a degree240 field extension','Q=\\zeta_{443520}','2026-10-16','T','Polylogarithmic root filters',
'How do all264 roots above q relate to the240 primitive conjugates, and which polylogarithm identity is actually valid?',
'The source records the integers384,264 and240. They measure different objects: base-field degree, the number of complex roots of a power equation, and extension degree. The root-of-unity filter makes this distinction explicit and supplies a convergent exact identity.',
['Set q=ζ₁₆₈₀, Q=ζ₄₄₃₅₂₀ and N=264, so Qᴺ=q. Let ξ=ζ_N.','For Re(s)>1 and |z|≤1, define Li_s(z)=Σ_(n≥1)zⁿ/nˢ by its absolutely convergent series.'],
'Full-root polylogarithm filter and primitive count',
'Σ_(r=0)^(N−1)Li_s(Qξʳ)=N^(1−s)Li_s(q). There are264 distinct roots of X²⁶⁴=q, but exactly240 of them are primitive order443520 roots, and [Q(Q):Q(q)]=240.',
'Absolute convergence permits the finite root sum to be interchanged with the series. The geometric sum Σ_rξ^(rn) is N when N divides n and zero otherwise. Writing n=Nℓ leaves NΣ_ℓQ^(Nℓ)/(Nℓ)ˢ=N^(1−s)Li_s(q), proving the filter with no branch continuation. All roots are Qξʳ=ζ₄₄₃₅₂₀^(1+1680r),0≤r<264. Their exponents are automatically coprime to2,3,5,7. The only possible extra common factor is11; exactly one residue class of r modulo11 makes1+1680r divisible by11, removing24 roots. Thus240 are primitive. Independently, φ(443520)=92160 and φ(1680)=384, giving the same extension degree240 by the tower law. The excluded r satisfy r≡4 mod11. The remaining240 exponents are exactly the cyclotomic automorphisms fixing q, so their sum is a relative field trace, whereas the264-term expression is a complete root filter. Filtering over only the240 primitive roots would therefore be a different sum and does not satisfy the displayed264-term identity unchanged.',
'Check two elementary specializations.',
'For N=2, Li₂(z)+Li₂(−z)=½Li₂(z²), by retaining the even series terms. Also q⁴²⁰=i, so Im Li₂(q⁴²⁰)=Σ_(m≥0)(−1)ᵐ/(2m+1)², the Catalan constant. These identities follow directly from convergent series and fix the phase conventions.',
'The power equation, primitive count and field degree are reconciled without mixing their meanings.',
'At Re(s)≤1, convergence and branches need separate analysis. The full-root filter does not independently prove a proposed240-to-two formula or every high-weight cancellation listed in the source.',
'Decompose the264-root sum into primitive and nonprimitive contributions by exact Möbius inversion; use those trace formulas to derive a correctly normalized primitive-field identity at each specified weight.',
[('NIST DLMF: polylogarithms','https://dlmf.nist.gov/25.12'),('NIST DLMF: finite Fourier sums','https://dlmf.nist.gov/27.10')])
add('The inverse-central-binomial series is an arcsine square','Raghava arcsine','2026-10-16','A','Exact special-function series',
'What exact identity produces the recovered π²/352800 value, and which algebraic argument is required?',
'The source lists an arcsine family alongside cyclotomic phases. The base identity has a direct differential-equation proof, avoiding a fitted numerical constant. The conductor1680 argument is stated explicitly, so the rational multiple of π² has a reproducible origin.',
['For |a|<2, define S(a)=Σ_(n≥1)a^(2n)/[n²·binom(2n,n)].','Use the principal real arcsine on[−1,1]; complex continuation is not needed for the worked example.'],
'Exact inverse-binomial identity',
'S(a)=2arcsin(a/2)² for real |a|≤2. In particular, for a=2sin(π/840), S(a)=π²/352800.',
'Let c_n=1/[n²binom(2n,n)]. The ratio c_(n+1)/c_n=n²/[(2n+2)(2n+1)] shows that S is analytic for |a|<2. Comparing coefficients gives (4−a²)S″−aS′=4, with S(0)=S′(0)=0: the constant term is8c₁=4, and all later coefficients cancel by the ratio. Set u=arcsin(a/2) and v=√(4−a²). For F=2u², u′=1/v gives F′=4u/v and F″=4/v²+4au/v³. Hence (4−a²)F″−aF′=4 with the same initial data. Uniqueness of the analytic power-series solution proves S=F. For endpoint convergence, the elementary bound binom(2n,n)≥4ⁿ/(2√n) follows by induction: its consecutive-ratio comparison reduces to (2n+1)²≥4n(n+1). Thus the endpoint terms are at most2/n^(3/2), a summable majorant. Dominated continuity extends the identity to a=±2. For the specified positive a, π/840 lies in the principal arcsine interval, so 2(π/840)² gives the asserted denominator.',
'Compare a=1 and the conductor1680 phase.',
'At a=1, S(1)=2(π/6)²=π²/18. Its first three coefficients are1/2,1/24,1/180. If q=ζ₁₆₈₀, then a=(q−q⁻¹)/q⁴²⁰=2sin(π/840), since q⁴²⁰=i. Thus the argument is an explicitly given algebraic number. For a certified finite truncation with q=a²/4<1, the same coefficient bound gives tail at most2q^(N+1)/[(N+1)^(3/2)(1−q)] after N terms. This is an actual error bound, rather than a match of displayed decimals.',
'The special value follows from an exact analytic identity and a fixed branch choice.',
'The proof does not establish all reported harmonic-weight families through weight131 or any unprovided cancellation table. An unspecified symbol a cannot be assigned this π² value without the stated sine relation.',
'Differentiate or integrate a parameterized hypergeometric identity under proved convergence bounds to generate specified harmonic-weight companions, keeping their algebraic arguments and branch conditions explicit.',
[('NIST DLMF: inverse trigonometric functions','https://dlmf.nist.gov/4.23'),('NIST DLMF: inverse-trigonometric further properties','https://dlmf.nist.gov/4.24')])
add('The order30 cyclotomic kernel has an exact Mellin zero and logarithmic companion','E8 kernel','2026-10-16','A','Mellin transforms and cyclotomic sums',
'Can the recovered weight-two cancellation and logarithmic constant be derived exactly from the specified kernel?',
'The kernel formula is sufficiently explicit for a full proof. Its cancellation comes from a divisor sum, not from a numerical integration or an assumed E₈ interpretation. Differentiating the Mellin transform also derives the stated logarithmic companion.',
['Let Φ₃₀(x)=x⁸+x⁷−x⁵−x⁴−x³+x+1 and K(x)=240x²⁹/(1−x³⁰)−Φ₃₀′(x)/Φ₃₀(x),0<x<1.','Let c₃₀(n)=Σ_(d|gcd(30,n))dμ(30/d). For Re(s)>1 set D(s)=Σ_(n≥1)[c₃₀(n)+240·1_(30|n)]/nˢ.'],
'Exact zero and logarithmic Mellin derivative',
'∫₀¹(−log x)K(x)dx=0 and ∫₀¹(−log x)log(−log x)K(x)dx=−π²[16log2+12log3+10log5]/180.',
'From the product of Φ₃₀ over its primitive roots, logarithmic differentiation gives −Φ′/Φ=Σ_(n≥1)c₃₀(n)x^(n−1) for |x|<1. The other kernel term contributes240 precisely when30 divides n. The coefficients are bounded, so integrating against (−log x)^(s−1) is justified absolutely for Re(s)>1 and gives Γ(s)D(s). Summing the divisor formula yields D(s)=ζ(s)F(s), where F(s)=240·30^(−s)+∏_(p∈{2,3,5})(p^(1−s)−1). At s=2, the product is−4/15 and240/900=4/15, hence F(2)=D(2)=0. Differentiating under the integral is legitimate near s=2 by the same absolute bound with an additional |log(−log x)| factor. The Γ′ term vanishes because D(2)=0. Convergence can also be seen directly: Φ₃₀(1)=1, so the logarithmic derivative stays bounded near one while the first kernel term is asymptotic to8/(1−x). Multiplication by−log x removes this pole, and the extra logarithm leaves an integrable log(1−x) singularity. Near zero the kernel is bounded, so its logarithmic moments are integrable there as well. Logarithmic differentiation of the finite product gives F′(2)=−(4/15)[log30+log2+½log3+¼log5]. Multiplying by ζ(2)=π²/6 produces the displayed companion.',
'Check the coefficient arithmetic behind the cancellation.',
'The eight divisors of30 give Σ_(d|30)μ(30/d)/d=−1+1/2+1/3+1/5−1/6−1/10−1/15+1/30=−4/15. The exact added coefficient240/30² is4/15. Their sum vanishes before any transcendental evaluation. The cancellation does not say K is zero: its limit at zero is−1, while it is positive and unbounded sufficiently near one. The integral balances genuinely nonzero regions of opposite sign.',
'Two recovered integral identities are fully proved with their convergence hypotheses.',
'The name E₈ for this scalar kernel requires a separate Coxeter/lattice identification. This proof does not certify every reported cancellation family or infer a physical spectrum from a Mellin zero.',
'For a prescribed conductor N and weight s₀>1, choose the compensating coefficient C=−N^(s₀)Σ_(d|N)μ(N/d)d^(1−s₀); then derive the exact zero and its higher logarithmic derivatives from the finite divisor polynomial.',
[('NIST DLMF: Ramanujan sums','https://dlmf.nist.gov/27.10'),('NIST DLMF: Euler products and Dirichlet series','https://dlmf.nist.gov/27.4')])
add('The five-phase logarithmic integral equals 955π³/867 exactly','955\\pi^3','2026-10-20','A','Fourier analysis and exact integrals',
'Can the recovered phase integral be proved, including its logarithmic singularities and rational coefficient?',
'The exact phase set makes this one of the strongest independently checkable October identities. Fourier analysis reduces the integral to a finite Bernoulli-polynomial sum. Logarithmic endpoint and interior singularities are square-integrable and are part of the proof, not exclusions from it.',
['Let S={0,1,2,4,11}, θ_s=2πs/17 and P(x)=∏_(s∈S)(2x−2cos θ_s).','Define I=∫_(−1)^1 log²|P(x)|/√(1−x²)dx, equivalently ∫₀^πlog²|P(cos t)|dt. Let B₂(u)=u²−u+1/6 and {u} denote fractional part.'],
'Exact five-phase integral',
'I=(955/867)π³. More generally I/π³=Σ_(s,r∈S)[B₂({(s−r)/17})+B₂({(s+r)/17})] for this convention of product factors.',
'The factorization 2cos t−2cos θ=e^(−it)(e^(it)−e^(iθ))(e^(it)−e^(−iθ)) shows that log|2cos t−2cos θ|=−2Σ_(n≥1)cos(nt)cos(nθ)/n in L²(0,π). One can justify the boundary Fourier series by radial regularization of log|1−re^(iu)| and L² convergence of its square-summable coefficients as r↑1. Local logarithmic singularities, including the doubled endpoint singularity when θ=0, are L², so the factorization agrees with this limit. Summing over S gives −2Σa_ncos(nt)/n, where a_n=Σ_scos(nθ_s). Parseval yields I=2πΣa_n²/n². The identity2cos u cos v=cos(u−v)+cos(u+v), followed by Σ_(n≥1)cos(2πnu)/n²=π²B₂({u}), gives the finite formula. Evaluating its fifty rational B₂ terms with denominator17² gives exactly955/867. Individual B₂ terms may be negative, but the full quadratic expression is nonnegative for every phase multiset by its Parseval representation. This also checks the factor of two and shows why estimating each pair contribution independently can obscure the positivity of the original integral.',
'Give a rational-only arithmetic certificate for the coefficient.',
'For residues k modulo17, B₂(k/17)=k²/289−k/17+1/6. The verifier loops over all25 ordered pairs (s,r), adds the difference and sum residues, and obtains Fraction(955,867). Root’s independent audit computes the same finite sum separately.',
'The integral is established analytically, and its final rational coefficient is independently replayable without numerical quadrature.',
'A different normalization of each product factor would add constants inside the logarithm and change the integral. No KZ or group-closure conclusion follows merely from this phase integral.',
'For any finite rational phase set, derive the corresponding Bernoulli sum and classify sets with equal quadratic logarithmic energy; add higher moments only after proving convergence and the required multiple Fourier convolution identities.',
[('NIST DLMF: Bernoulli Fourier expansions','https://dlmf.nist.gov/24.8')])
add('Determinant twists explain the claimed compact group form but do not prove density','h=1/34 E8 KZ','2026-10-20','A','Braid representations and compact groups',
'Which consequences of the recovered braid and determinant relations can be proved without the missing KZ matrices?',
'The source asserts dense SU(5) monodromy and supplies braid, central-phase and determinant relations. The absent residues and matrices prevent an independent closure proof. Nevertheless the determinant twist, triangle-group quotient and structure of the proposed full compact group are exact conditional consequences that can be proved completely.',
['Let ζ=exp(2πi/17). Assume unitary5×5 matrices R₁,R₂ satisfy the braid relation, det R_i=ζ⁹ and (R₁R₂)³=ζ⁴I.','Set T_i=ζ⁵R_i and G={g∈U(5):det(g)¹⁷=1}. The hypothesis that a generated subgroup is dense is separate from these finite relations.'],
'Exact twist and split determinant group',
'T_i∈SU(5), they satisfy the braid relation, and (T₁T₂)³=I. If additionally T_i³⁴=I, they define a quotient of Δ(2,3,34). Moreover G≅SU(5)×μ₁₇ as compact groups, via (S,ξ)↦ξS.',
'Twisting a5×5 matrix by ζ⁵ multiplies its determinant by ζ²⁵; thus det T_i=ζ³⁴=1. Both sides of the braid relation gain the same scalar ζ¹⁵. The central relation gains ζ³⁰, cancelling ζ⁴ because ζ³⁴=1. Put a=T₁T₂T₁ and b=T₁T₂. The braid relation gives a²=b³=I, and b⁻¹a=T₁, so the extra relation T₁³⁴=I gives the triangle presentation with orders dividing2,3,34. For the group decomposition, ξ∈μ₁₇ has determinant ξ⁵. Since5 is invertible modulo17, this scalar determinant map is an automorphism; its inverse exponent is7. Given g∈G, set ξ=det(g)⁷ and S=ξ⁻¹g. Then det S=det(g)^(1−35)=1. This is a continuous inverse to (S,ξ)↦ξS, and the intersection SU(5)∩μ₁₇I is trivial, proving the product isomorphism.',
'Check phase consistency before any matrix computation.',
'The determinant of (R₁R₂)³ is ζ⁵⁴=ζ³, while det(ζ⁴I)=ζ²⁰=ζ³, so the reported relations are mutually compatible at determinant level. The twist makes both determinants and the central cube exactly one.',
'The arithmetic structure of the proposed compact closure is verified conditionally, with its missing density step explicit.',
'Finite order, irreducibility or a finite list of nonzero projector blocks does not alone prove density in SU(5). The actual matrices, unitarizing form and exclusion of finite and proper closed subgroups are not recovered; no claimed holonomy residual or Lean certificate is replayed.',
'Publish exact A,B or certified monodromy matrices, verify the unitary form and braid relations, and give an explicit Lie-algebra or subgroup-classification certificate for the connected closure before identifying the full determinant extension.',
[('Brochier: cyclotomic KZ monodromy','https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.4285')])
